  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nEarly morning surface observations reveal patchy dense valley \nfog across sheltered river basins. Will likely issue a special\nweather statement in the next couple hours for patchy fog for\nthe morning commute. Fog and low stratus will burn off by 9 AM.\nA capping inversion centered near H825 beneath a diffuse \nelevated mixed layer will inhibit surface-based convective \ninitiation through mid-afternoon. Daytime highs will reach the \nupper 80s to lower 90s across the lowlands and the upper 70s to \nmiddle 80s across higher terrain. \n\nConsiderable uncertainly remains regarding convective evolution\nwith one more more ridge riding complexes likely to near/cross\nthe region through Tuesday morning.\n\nLate this afternoon into tonight, a convective complex is \nexpected to organize over the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes \nwithin a favorable kinematic regime featuring 35 to 50KTs of \n0-6km bulk shear. Driven by an embedded shortwave in \nnorthwesterly H500 flow, this complex will track southeastward \nalong a sharp buoyancy gradient. While the core of this activity\nis favored to pass west of the forecast area into central \nKentucky, the eastern fringe may brush southeast Ohio, northeast\nKentucky, and western West Virginia between 9 PM to 1 AM. \n\nA second convective complex is progged to move out of the Great\nLakes early Tuesday morning ahead of an amplifying H500\nvorticity maximum and a 45 to 50KT mid-level speed max. \nThis early morning complex brings a continued threat for \ngusty winds and heavy downpours through mid-morning Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nConsiderable forecast uncertainty exists for Tuesday afternoon,\nas severe potential remains highly contingent on morning\nconvective clearing and thermodynamic recovery. If the morning\nstorm complex exits quickly enough to allow 3 to 5 hours of\nsolar insolation, destabilization will yield MLCAPE values of\n1500 to 2000 J/kg ahead of an advancing surface cold front.\n\nThere is also a substantial run to run inconsistency in the \nplacement of the strongest wind field for Tuesday morning, \ngenerally fluctuating anywhere from central KY to central VA \nover the last 24 hours. The main threat with this activity would\nbe damaging wind gusts as storms should grow upscale fairly \nrapidly. Should the higher end kinematic wind field materialize\ncould certainly envision some embedded super cellular structures\nwith an attendant marginal tornado threat. At this time we \nremain outlooked by SPC in day 2 for wind/hail only which seems\nreasonable given the substantial uncertainty.\n\nAlthough this activity should be fairly progressive \ngiven the forcing, rainfall rates exceeding 1 to 2 inches per \nhour could trigger rapid basin response and localized flash \nflooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage Tuesday night, H850 cold air\nadvection and building surface high pressure from Canada will\nusher in a significantly drier airmass for Wednesday through\nFriday. Precipitable water values will drop to between 0.75 and\n1.00 inch, near the 10th percentile for late July. High\ntemperatures will reset to near-normal values, ranging from the\nupper 70s in the Northeast Mountains to the mid 80s in the\nlowlands. Due to ongoing large-scale wildfires in the Inland\nNorthwest and Ontario, northwesterly flow aloft will advect\nelevated smoke into the region, leading to periods of hazy skies\nthrough the end of the week.",
